Keep editing when the file operand cannot be read.
When the file named on the command line could not be read, ed printed
the error and exited at once without reading any command, so "ed
newfile" could not be used to create a file, and a script fed to it
was silently ignored. Report the error and continue with an empty
buffer that remembers the name, as other implementations of ed do, so
that w creates the file.

---
 editors/ed.c |    6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/ed.c
+++ b/editors/ed.c
@@ -1019,9 +1019,9 @@
 
 	if (argv[0]) {
 		fileName = xstrdup(argv[0]);
-		if (!readLines(fileName, 1)) {
-			return EXIT_SUCCESS;
-		}
+		/* If it can't be read, start with an empty buffer
+		 * under that name, so that a new file can be created */
+		readLines(fileName, 1);
 		dirty = FALSE;
 	}
 
